    {
      "id": "sec-4",
      "title": "The Law of Refraction",
      "module": "m42459",
      "est_minutes": 12,
      "locator": "CNXML module m42459",
      "objectives": [
        "Determine the index of refraction, given the speed of light in a medium."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        {
          "claim": "A converging lens forms an image at the same place whatever the object distance.",
          "correction": "The image distance changes with the object distance, and the lens equation gives it."
        }
      ],
      "summary": [
        "The changing of a light ray’s direction when it passes through variations in matter is called refraction.",
        "The speed of light in vacuum c=2.99792458×108 m/s≈3.00×108 m/s.",
        "Index of refraction n=cv, where v is the speed of light in the material, c is the speed of light in vacuum, and n is the index of refraction."
      ],
